      en: "Kalēti Ridge is a series of parallel moraine ridges in western Latvia, in the historic region of Courland, reaching 190 m in elevation. It extends 15 km north-south. Formed during the Weichselian glaciation, the ridges consist of sandy-loamy till. Poor soils support a heathland ecosystem with juniper, heather, and lichens. The ridge is part of the Kalēti Protected Area, established to conserve open-habitat birds like the red-backed shrike and woodlark. Historically used for sheep grazing, the area now faces afforestation, requiring active management. Several educational trails run along the ridge, explaining glacial geomorphology and ecology.",
